const SKILLS=[
{id:'shaking',t:'Shaking',cat:'Mixing',ic:'shaker',sum:'The fast way to mix, chill and dilute, with a lively, frothy texture.',
 what:'Shaking throws the drink and ice around a sealed shaker. In a few seconds it mixes the ingredients, chills them, adds water from melting ice and beats in tiny air bubbles. Those bubbles are why shaken drinks look slightly cloudy and feel lighter in the mouth.',
 when:'Use it for any drink with citrus juice, cream, egg white, purée or other cloudy or thick ingredients. A useful rule: if everything in the drink is clear (spirits, vermouth, bitters), stir it; if anything is cloudy, shake it.',
 steps:['Chill your serving glass first (see Chilling glassware).','Measure the ingredients into the smaller tin or glass of a two-piece shaker.','Fill the larger tin about two-thirds with solid, cold cubed ice.','Put the small tin in at a slight angle and tap the top firmly with the heel of your hand to seal it.','Hold both ends, small tin pointing towards you, and shake hard for roughly 12\u201315 seconds, until the outside feels very cold.','Tap the larger tin where the two meet to break the seal, then strain into the glass (fine strain if the drink is served without ice).'],
 mist:['Too little ice \u2013 it melts quickly and you get a warm, watery drink. Fill the tin well.','Shaking with crushed or hollow ice when the recipe does not ask for it \u2013 dilution goes up fast and is hard to control.','Pouring the shaking ice into the glass. It is broken and half-melted; serve over fresh ice instead.','Shaking spirit-only drinks such as a Manhattan by habit \u2013 it works, but they turn cloudy and lose their silky texture.'],
 ex:['Daiquiri','Margarita','Cosmopolitan','Espresso Martini','Sidecar','Last Word'],src:['dg_shake','dg_svs'],rel:['dry-shake','whip-shake','double-straining','ice']},
{id:'dry-shake',t:'Dry shake & reverse dry shake',cat:'Mixing',ic:'shaker',sum:'Two-stage shaking that builds a thick foam on egg white and cream drinks.',
 what:'A dry shake simply means shaking without ice. It is paired with a normal iced shake to whip egg white, aquafaba or cream into a fine foam. In a classic dry shake you shake without ice first, then with ice. A reverse dry shake flips the order: shake with ice first, strain off the ice, then shake again without it.',
 when:'Sours and fizzes that contain egg white or a vegan alternative, when you want a tall, stable head. Difford\u2019s Guide finds that the reverse method gives the most foam; some bartenders prefer the classic order, so it is worth trying both.',
 steps:['Put all the ingredients, including the egg white, into the small tin.','Reverse method: add ice, seal and shake hard for about 12\u201315 seconds.','Strain the liquid into the other (empty) tin and throw the ice away.','Seal again and shake hard without ice for about 10 seconds. This is where the foam builds.','Fine strain into a chilled glass and let the foam settle for a moment before garnishing.','Classic method: do steps 4 and 2 the other way round \u2013 shake without ice first, then add ice, shake again and fine strain.'],
 mist:['Not holding the tins firmly. Without ice the seal is weaker, so keep a hand on both ends.','Skipping the fine strainer \u2013 it catches bits of egg and stops the foam looking lumpy.','Using old eggs. Raw egg carries a small food-safety risk; use very fresh eggs or pasteurised egg white.'],
 ex:['Pisco Sour','Clover Club','White Lady','Whiskey Sour'],src:['dg_dry','punch_rds'],rel:['shaking','double-straining']},
{id:'whip-shake',t:'Whip shake',cat:'Mixing',ic:'shaker',sum:'A short shake with a little crushed ice, for drinks served over crushed ice.',
 what:'A whip shake uses only a small amount of small ice \u2013 a scoop of crushed ice or a few pebbles \u2013 and you shake until it has more or less disappeared. You get chilling, mixing and plenty of air, but the dilution stays limited because there is only a little ice to melt.',
 when:'Drinks that will be served over crushed ice, such as many tiki-style drinks, so they are not watered down twice. It also helps froth up cream or egg drinks.',
 steps:['Measure the ingredients into the shaker tin.','Add a small scoop of crushed ice or a few small pieces of ice \u2013 not a full tin.','Seal and shake hard until you can no longer hear the ice rattling.','Pour the whole contents into the glass (there is little or nothing to strain).','Top up with fresh crushed ice and garnish.'],
 mist:['Adding too much ice \u2013 you end up shaking for ages and the drink is over-diluted.','Using large cubes \u2013 they take far longer to melt than small ice.','Whip shaking a drink that is meant to be served straight up \u2013 it will not be cold enough without a full iced shake.'],
 ex:['Mai Tai','Bramble','Grasshopper'],exNote:'The library recipes use a standard shake; these are drinks where a whip shake is a good alternative.',src:['manual_whip','dg_shake'],rel:['shaking','ice']},
{id:'stirring',t:'Stirring',cat:'Mixing',ic:'spoon',sum:'Chills and dilutes while keeping a drink crystal clear and silky.',
 what:'Stirring moves the ice and liquid around a mixing glass with a long bar spoon. It chills and dilutes the drink without adding air bubbles, so the result is clear with a smooth, weighty texture. It works more slowly than shaking.',
 when:'Drinks made only of clear ingredients \u2013 spirits, vermouths, fortified wines, liqueurs and bitters \u2013 such as the Martini, Manhattan, Negroni and Old Fashioned.',
 steps:['Chill the mixing glass and serving glass (in the freezer, or with ice and water you then tip away).','Measure the ingredients into the mixing glass and fill it about two-thirds with cold cubed ice.','Hold the spoon lightly near the top, with the shaft between your middle and ring fingers.','Slide the back of the spoon down the inside of the glass and keep it against the wall.','Move the spoon round the glass with your fingers, not your arm, so the ice spins smoothly. Stir for about 30\u201345 seconds.','Taste a drop from the spoon, then strain into the chilled glass \u2013 onto fresh ice if the recipe says so.'],
 mist:['Stirring for only a few seconds \u2013 the drink is warm and harsh. Taste and keep going if needed.','Using wet, small or crushed ice \u2013 it melts quickly and waters the drink down.','Pouring the stirring ice into the glass. Use fresh ice for serving.','Stirring drinks with juice or cream \u2013 they need a shake to mix properly.'],
 ex:['Dry Martini','Manhattan','Negroni','Old Fashioned','Rob Roy','Boulevardier','Sazerac'],src:['dg_stir','dg_svs'],rel:['chilling-glassware','ice','throwing']},
{id:'building',t:'Building',cat:'Mixing',ic:'glass',sum:'Making a drink straight in its serving glass \u2013 the method for highballs.',
 what:'Building means pouring the ingredients directly into the glass the drink is served in, usually over ice, and giving it at most a gentle stir. There is no shaker or mixing glass.',
 when:'Highballs and long drinks with a fizzy top-up (cola, soda, ginger beer, tonic, sparkling wine) and simple drinks poured over ice.',
 steps:['Use a chilled glass and fill it right to the top with fresh, solid ice.','Measure in the spirit and any other still ingredients.','Top up with a cold mixer, pouring gently down the inside of the glass or a bar spoon to keep the bubbles.','Lift the ingredients together with one or two gentle turns of a bar spoon.','Add the garnish and serve straight away.'],
 mist:['Only half-filling the glass with ice. More ice keeps the drink colder and it melts more slowly than a few cubes.','Using warm mixers or a bottle that has been open for days \u2013 the drink goes flat and dilutes faster.','Stirring hard after adding the fizz \u2013 it knocks out the bubbles.'],
 ex:['Cuba Libre','Moscow Mule','Paloma','Americano','Aperol Spritz','Dark and Stormy','White Russian'],src:['dg_highball','dg_ice'],rel:['ice','chilling-glassware']},
{id:'throwing',t:'Throwing',cat:'Mixing',ic:'shaker',sum:'Pouring a drink back and forth between two tins from a height.',
 what:'Throwing mixes a drink by pouring it in a long stream from one ice-filled vessel held high into another held low, then back again several times. It chills and dilutes like stirring but adds fine bubbles, which can lift the aroma and texture. The technique was kept alive in the 20th century at Boadas in Barcelona, whose founder brought it from Havana.',
 when:'Mainly drinks built on wine-based ingredients such as vermouth and sherry, where a little air opens up the aroma. A gentler cousin, rolling (pouring between tins without the height), is often used for the Bloody Mary.',
 steps:['Use two large shaker tins. Put the ingredients in one with ice filling it about two-thirds.','Hold a Hawthorn or julep strainer in the top tin so the ice stays behind.','Start with both tins together up high, then pour slowly while lowering the catching tin, letting the stream stretch out.','Keep your eyes on the catching tin, and tilt it a little so the stream hits the inside wall.','Bring the tins back together, pour the drink back over the ice and repeat four or five times.','Strain into a chilled glass.'],
 mist:['Starting with the tins apart \u2013 you will spill. Start close and let the bottom tin drop.','Looking at the top tin instead of the bottom one.','Using too much ice in the top tin, which makes it hard to control.','Practise with water first.'],
 ex:['Bloody Mary','Manhattan'],exNote:'The library Bloody Mary is rolled; the Manhattan is listed as stirred, and throwing is an alternative to try.',src:['dg_throw','oc_roll'],rel:['stirring']},
{id:'double-straining',t:'Double straining',cat:'Mixing',ic:'drop',sum:'Two strainers at once, for a perfectly smooth drink.',
 what:'Double (or fine) straining means pouring the drink through the shaker\u2019s main strainer and, at the same time, through a small fine-mesh sieve held over the glass. The sieve catches ice chips, fruit pulp, seeds and herb fragments.',
 when:'Shaken drinks served straight up (with no ice in the glass), drinks with muddled fruit or herbs, and egg white drinks.',
 steps:['Hold the Hawthorn strainer firmly over the shaker tin with your forefinger.','Hold a fine strainer in your other hand over the chilled glass.','Pour through both. Tap the fine strainer gently if it clogs.','Throw away whatever collects in the sieve.'],
 mist:['Skipping it on drinks served up \u2013 floating ice chips keep diluting the drink and spoil the texture.','Pushing pulp through the mesh with a spoon.','Fine straining a drink onto crushed ice when there is no need \u2013 the ice already hides any chips.'],
 ex:['Daiquiri','Cosmopolitan','Espresso Martini','Pisco Sour','Last Word'],src:['dg_fine','dg_strain'],rel:['shaking','dry-shake','muddling']},
{id:'muddling',t:'Muddling',cat:'Mixing',ic:'leaf',sum:'Gently pressing fruit, herbs or sugar to release flavour.',
 what:'Muddling uses a muddler \u2013 a short, blunt pestle \u2013 to press ingredients in the base of a shaker or sturdy glass, like a pestle and mortar. It releases juice from fruit and aromatic oils from herbs and peel.',
 when:'Drinks with fresh fruit, citrus wedges or herbs, such as the Mojito, Caipirinha and Mint Julep.',
 steps:['Use the base of a stainless-steel shaker tin or a thick-bottomed glass.','Add the ingredients to be muddled, plus any sugar or syrup, which helps to grind and extract.','Press down with a gentle twist, like using a pestle. For citrus, press firmly until the juice runs.','For mint and soft herbs, pick the leaves off the stalks and press only lightly \u2013 just enough to bruise them and release the smell.','Add the remaining ingredients and ice, then shake or build as the recipe says.'],
 mist:['Shredding mint \u2013 it turns bitter and grassy, and bits get stuck in your teeth. Bruise, do not grind.','Muddling hard fruit in a thin glass, which can crack it.','Muddling too long \u2013 a few firm presses are usually enough.'],
 ex:['Mojito','Caipirinha','Mint Julep'],src:['dg_muddle','se_mint'],rel:['double-straining']},
{id:'layering',t:'Layering & floating',cat:'Mixing',ic:'drop',sum:'Stacking liquids in bands, or floating one on top.',
 what:'Layering builds a drink in separate bands of colour by pouring each liquid gently on top of the last. It works because liquids with more sugar (and less alcohol) are heavier and sink, while lighter, stronger ones sit on top. A float is a single final layer poured onto the surface of a finished drink.',
 when:'Layered shots such as the B-52, and drinks finished with a float \u2013 red wine on a New York Sour, cream on an Irish Coffee, a smoky whisky on a Penicillin.',
 steps:['Work out the order: syrups and sweet liqueurs at the bottom, lighter liqueurs next, spirits on top. Cream can float on many liqueurs.','Pour the heaviest ingredient into the glass first.','Hold a bar spoon just above the surface, back of the bowl up, touching the inside of the glass.','Pour the next liquid very slowly over the back of the spoon so it spreads across the surface.','Raise the spoon as the level rises and repeat for each layer.'],
 mist:['Pouring too fast \u2013 the layers punch through and mix.','Getting the order wrong. If a layer sinks, it was heavier than the one below.','Using a narrow, wide-rimmed glass for shots \u2013 tall, slim glasses make each layer look thicker.'],
 ex:['B-52','New York Sour','Penicillin','Irish Coffee','Bramble'],src:['dg_layer','wp_pousse'],rel:['measuring']},
{id:'measuring',t:'Measuring & jiggering',cat:'Basics',ic:'flask',sum:'Accurate measures are the easiest way to make every drink taste right.',
 what:'A jigger is a small double-ended measure, usually with marks inside for smaller amounts. Measuring every ingredient keeps a drink\u2019s balance of strong, sweet and sour the same every time, and keeps costs under control.',
 when:'Every drink. In UK bars, gin, rum, vodka and whisky served on their own must be sold in 25 ml or 35 ml measures (or multiples), and the bar must use one size or the other; drinks mixed from three or more liquids are exempt.',
 steps:['Choose a jigger with the sizes you use most (this app works in ml).','Hold it level between your thumb and first two fingers, close to the top of the tin.','Pour until the liquid is right at the brim or the line \u2013 not just below.','Tip it into the tin with a quick turn of the wrist so it empties fully.','Rinse between sticky ingredients like syrups and cream liqueurs.','Many bartenders add the cheapest ingredients first, so a mistake wastes less.'],
 mist:['Holding the jigger at an angle, so it looks full but is short.','Free-pouring without practice \u2013 small errors in sweet or sour ingredients really show.','Mixing up US ounces and ml. One US fl oz is about 29.6 ml; the app rounds to 30 ml.'],
 ex:['Negroni','Last Word','Daiquiri','Margarita'],src:['dg_measure','gov_measure'],rel:['syrups']},
{id:'ice',t:'Ice types & dilution',cat:'Basics',ic:'ice',sum:'Ice is an ingredient: the type you use decides how cold and how watery a drink gets.',
 what:'Ice chills a drink and, as it melts, adds water. That water is part of the recipe \u2013 it softens alcohol and opens up flavour. Difford\u2019s Guide measures about a quarter extra volume from a normal shake and a little less from stirring. Smaller pieces have more surface, so they chill and melt faster.',
 when:'Solid cubes (around 2.5 cm) for shaking, stirring and most drinks on the rocks. Crushed ice for juleps, swizzles and tiki drinks where lots of chill and dilution is wanted. A large cube or block for spirit-forward drinks like the Old Fashioned, where you want slow melting.',
 steps:['Use cold, solid, dry-looking cubes. Avoid hollow or thin wafer-shaped ice.','Keep ice in a clean, covered container away from strong-smelling food \u2013 it picks up smells.','Fill shakers and glasses generously; more ice actually means less melting.','To crush ice, wrap cubes in a clean tea towel or Lewis bag and hit them with a muddler or rolling pin.','If your ice is straight from a very cold freezer, let it sit for a few minutes, or add a little chilled water, so the drink is not under-diluted.'],
 mist:['Reusing ice from the shaker or mixing glass in the serving glass.','Using wet, half-melted ice, which over-dilutes.','Shaking with crushed ice when the recipe asks for cubes.'],
 ex:['Mint Julep','Old Fashioned','Bramble','Mai Tai','Tom Collins'],src:['dg_ice','dg_water'],rel:['shaking','stirring','building']},
{id:'chilling-glassware',t:'Chilling glassware',cat:'Basics',ic:'glass',sum:'A cold glass keeps a drink at its best for longer.',
 what:'Drinks served straight up, without ice, start to warm up the moment they hit the glass. Starting with a cold glass slows that down. For hot drinks it works the other way: warm the glass first.',
 when:'Especially for stemmed glasses \u2013 Martini, coupe, Nick & Nora and flute \u2013 but any glass benefits. Warm the glass for hot drinks like the Irish Coffee and Hot Toddy.',
 steps:['Best: put the glasses in the freezer for 20\u201330 minutes before use.','Quick way: fill the glass with ice (crushed is best) and top with water while you make the drink.','Tip out the ice and water just before you pour, and shake out any drops.','Hot drinks: put a metal bar spoon in the glass, fill with hot water, tip it out, pour in the drink, then remove the spoon.','Hold stemmed glasses by the stem or base, never the bowl, so your hand does not warm them.'],
 mist:['Leaving ice water in the glass and pouring the drink on top of the leftover water.','Pouring boiling liquid into a cold glass \u2013 it can crack.','Forgetting to chill until the drink is already made.'],
 ex:['Dry Martini','Manhattan','Daiquiri','French 75','Irish Coffee','Hot Toddy'],src:['dg_glass'],rel:['stirring','ice']},
{id:'syrups',t:'Making syrups',cat:'Prep',ic:'jar',sum:'Simple, rich, honey and demerara syrups \u2013 the bar\u2019s basic sweeteners.',
 what:'Sugar does not dissolve easily in cold drinks, so bars use syrups: sugar already dissolved in water. Simple syrup is equal parts sugar and water (1:1). Rich syrup is twice as much sugar as water (2:1), so it is sweeter and dilutes less. Honey syrup thins honey with warm water so it mixes cold. Demerara syrup uses demerara sugar for a richer, toffee-like taste.',
 when:'Simple syrup for most sours and fresh drinks. Rich syrup where you want less water or more texture, such as the Old Fashioned. Honey syrup for honey drinks (the Bee\u2019s Knees and Penicillin family). Demerara with aged rum and whisky.',
 steps:['Weigh or measure the sugar and water: 1:1 for simple, 2:1 for rich. Demerara can be made either way.','Warm the water gently in a pan and stir in the sugar until fully dissolved. It does not need to boil.','For honey syrup, stir honey into warm water until smooth \u2013 1:1 is common; some recipes use a little more honey.','Let it cool, pour into a clean bottle, label it with the type and date, and keep it in the fridge.','Rich syrup keeps longer than 1:1. Throw any syrup away if it turns cloudy or smells off.'],
 mist:['Boiling hard \u2013 water evaporates and the syrup becomes richer than the recipe expects.','Swapping rich syrup for simple at the same measure \u2013 the drink will be too sweet. Use less.','Not labelling bottles, so you cannot tell 1:1 from 2:1.'],
 ex:['Daiquiri','Old Fashioned','Mojito','Penicillin','Tom Collins'],src:['lq_syrups','lq_rich'],rel:['oleo-saccharum','measuring']},
{id:'oleo-saccharum',t:'Oleo saccharum',cat:'Prep',ic:'citrus',sum:'Citrus-oil sugar \u2013 the traditional fragrant sweetener for punch.',
 what:'Oleo saccharum (roughly \u201coil sugar\u201d) is made by leaving citrus peels in sugar. The sugar draws out the oils from the peel and turns into a strongly scented syrup. It has been part of punch recipes for centuries.',
 when:'Punches and large-format drinks, or any drink where you want a big lemon or orange aroma. Use it in place of some or all of the plain sugar or syrup.',
 steps:['Wash unwaxed lemons (or oranges or grapefruit) and peel them with a vegetable peeler, leaving the white pith behind.','Put the peels in a bowl or jar and add sugar \u2013 roughly a generous handful (about 50 g) for each lemon\u2019s peel.','Toss well, then press the peels into the sugar with a muddler to start releasing the oil.','Cover and leave for at least an hour, ideally a few hours or overnight, stirring now and then. The sugar will turn into a syrup.','Strain out the peels, pressing them to get every drop, and keep the syrup in the fridge.'],
 mist:['Leaving lots of pith on the peel \u2013 it makes the syrup bitter.','Using waxed fruit, which stops the oil coming out cleanly.','Treating it like simple syrup \u2013 it is very sweet, so taste and adjust.'],
 ex:['Planter\u2019s Punch','Sangria'],exNote:'These punches do not use oleo saccharum in the library recipes; they are good drinks to try it in.',src:['wp_oleo','se_oleo'],rel:['syrups']},
{id:'fat-washing',t:'Fat-washing',cat:'Advanced',ic:'bottle',sum:'Giving a spirit the flavour of butter, bacon or oil \u2013 then removing the fat.',
 what:'Fat-washing mixes a spirit with a melted fat, lets the flavour move into the spirit, then freezes the mix so the fat sets hard and can be lifted off. The spirit keeps the taste and a little extra body. It became famous through the Benton\u2019s Old Fashioned, a bacon-fat-washed bourbon drink created by Don Lee at PDT in New York in 2007.',
 when:'When you want a savoury or rich flavour that would be hard to infuse any other way \u2013 brown butter in rum, bacon fat in bourbon, sesame or olive oil in gin.',
 steps:['Melt the fat. PDT\u2019s published ratio for its bacon bourbon is about 1 US fl oz (30 ml) of fat to one 750 ml bottle.','Pour the warm fat into the spirit in a wide container, which gives more contact, and stir.','Leave at room temperature for a few hours, stirring now and then (PDT uses about four hours).','Put the container in the freezer until the fat is completely solid \u2013 PDT freezes for around 12 hours.','Lift off the solid fat, then strain the spirit through a coffee filter or muslin.','Bottle, label and keep it in the fridge.'],
 mist:['Not freezing for long enough \u2013 the fat stays soft and gets into the bottle.','Using too much fat, so the spirit tastes greasy.','Skipping the final filter \u2013 small bits of fat spoil the look and texture.'],
 ex:['Old Fashioned'],exNote:'The Benton\u2019s Old Fashioned is an Old Fashioned made with bacon-fat-washed bourbon and maple syrup.',src:['se_fat','eater_benton'],rel:['infusions','milk-punch']},
{id:'milk-punch',t:'Milk & clarified punch',cat:'Advanced',ic:'drop',sum:'Curdling milk into a punch, then straining it crystal clear.',
 what:'Clarified milk punch is made by adding milk to an acidic punch (spirit, citrus, sugar, often tea or spice). The acid curdles the milk; the curds then trap the cloudy parts of the drink and are strained out. What is left is a clear, silky drink that keeps much longer than fresh citrus drinks. The technique dates back to the 1700s.',
 when:'Batch drinks for parties, drinks you want to make ahead, or any punch you would like clear and smoother. There is also a non-clarified style of milk punch \u2013 brandy or bourbon with milk, sugar and nutmeg.',
 steps:['Mix the punch base following your recipe: spirit, citrus juice, sweetener and any tea or spices.','Measure the whole milk your recipe calls for into a separate large container.','Pour the punch into the milk (most modern guides say this way round gives better curds), stir once gently, and stop.','Leave it undisturbed until clear liquid separates from the curds \u2013 many recipes rest it in the fridge for several hours.','Strain through a coffee filter or cheesecloth. Pour the first cloudy run back through: the bed of curds does the fine filtering.','Bottle, label and keep in the fridge.'],
 mist:['Stirring after the curds form \u2013 it breaks them up and makes straining slow and cloudy.','Using skimmed milk \u2013 whole milk gives firmer curds and a richer texture.','Rushing the filtering. It is slow; let gravity do it.'],
 ex:[],src:['wp_milk','punch_milk'],rel:['fat-washing','infusions']},
{id:'infusions',t:'Infusions',cat:'Prep',ic:'jar',sum:'Steeping fruit, spice, chilli or tea in a spirit to give it new flavour.',
 what:'An infusion is a spirit that has had something soaked in it \u2013 citrus peel, chilli, vanilla, fruit, tea or herbs \u2013 then strained out. Alcohol is very good at pulling out flavour, so infusions can be quick.',
 when:'When you want a signature twist on a classic, such as chilli tequila in a Margarita or a tea-infused gin.',
 steps:['Use a clean glass jar with a tight lid and a decent mid-priced spirit.','Add the flavouring. Cut fruit and chillies to expose more surface.','Seal and keep in a cool, dark place, shaking now and then.','Taste often. Chillies and citrus peel can be ready within hours; fruit and whole spices usually take longer.','When it tastes right, strain straight away through a fine sieve, then a coffee filter for clarity.','Bottle, label with what and when, and keep fresh-fruit infusions in the fridge.'],
 mist:['Leaving it too long \u2013 peel turns bitter, chilli becomes painfully hot.','Not tasting \u2013 times vary a lot between batches.','Keeping an infusion that smells off or shows mould. Throw it away.'],
 ex:['Margarita','Bloody Mary'],exNote:'Ideas only: these library recipes use plain spirits.',src:['punch_inf'],rel:['fat-washing','oleo-saccharum']},
{id:'rimming',t:'Rimming a glass',cat:'Garnish',ic:'citrus',sum:'A neat salt or sugar edge on the outside of the glass.',
 what:'A rim is a thin coat of salt, sugar or another seasoning on the edge of the glass. Each sip picks some up, so it changes the taste of the drink as you go.',
 when:'Margaritas and other tequila drinks (salt), the Salty Dog (salt), and drinks like the Lemon Drop or Sidecar (sugar).',
 steps:['Spread the salt or sugar on a small plate.','Run a cut citrus wedge round the outside edge of the glass only \u2013 not the inside.','Hold the glass at an angle and roll the outer edge through the salt or sugar, rather than dipping it straight down.','Tap the glass gently to knock off loose grains.','Rim half or two-thirds of the glass if you want to give the drinker a choice.','Do it before you make the drink so the rim can dry.'],
 mist:['Dipping the glass upside down \u2013 salt gets inside and falls into the drink.','Using coarse rock salt \u2013 a flaky salt is the usual choice.','A rim that is too thick, which overwhelms the drink.'],
 ex:['Margarita','Paloma','Salty Dog','Lemon Drop','Sidecar'],src:['dg_marg'],rel:['chilling-glassware'],gar:'rims'}
];
const SK_CATS=['Basics','Mixing','Prep','Advanced','Garnish'];
